x's paired\n106\t   `event_msg`/`response_item` twins.\n107\t\n108\tTiers 2 and 3 are what the adapter abstraction exists for. Discovering that a\n109\tfield assumed to be tier 1 is really tier 2 is a contract change worth\n110\trecording here, not a quiet workaround inside one adapter.\n111\t\n112\t- Pin the CLI versions the writer was validated against; on an unknown version\n113\t  warn and offer bootstrap.\n114\t- Write only new session files under the CLI's own session directory; never\n115\t  modify existing native sessions.\n116\t- Represent foreign tool calls faithfully (or as clearly-labeled text when the\n117\t  target rejects unknown tool schemas — see per-adapter spec notes).\n118\t- Propagate the source model id into fabricated assistant envelopes verbatim\n119\t  **where the target can resolve it** (decided 2026-07-21, amended 2026-08-06).\n120\t  If the target recognizes it — e.g. the user registered matching external\n121\t  models in Claude Code — the session restores seamlessly. If it does not, write\n122\t  a value the target *can* resolve and disclose the real source model in the\n123\t  import notice; do not leave an unresolvable id in a field the target uses to\n124\t  dispatch. The original rule (\"never substitute a recognized-but-false id\")\n125\t  was written to keep the transcript honest and instead produced sessions that\n126\t  could not be continued at all; honesty belongs in the disclosure, not in a\n127\t  dispatch field.\n128\t\n129\tReverse-engineered, empirically verified format specs (pinned versions,\n130\tminimal working schemas, unknowns): [docs/specs/claude-session-format.md](specs/claude-session-format.md),\n131\t[docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md](specs/codex-rollout-format.md), and\n\n... [148 lines truncated] ...","type":"edited_text_file"},"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"parentUuid":"12a592a6-706f-4e3d-8867-f227ca890ca9","sessionId":"4fc5e727-a1d5-4399-a448-9d24a0ce9c15","sessionKind":"bg","session_id":"4fc5e727-a1d5-4399-a448-9d24a0ce9c15","timestamp":"2026-08-11T11:00:11.942Z","type":"attachment","userType":"external","uuid":"9a6bbd33-7d85-4f2b-a90d-3d2260e1a135","version":"2.1.227"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-11T11:42:59.035Z","schema":"annals/v1","stream":{"id":"claude-code:4fc5e727-a1d5-4399-a448-9d24a0ce9c15","seq":693}}
{"content":{"attachment":{"filename":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md","snippet":"1\t# opencode native session / import format — reverse-engineering spec\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: enable `turnbridge` to FABRICATE an opencode session that `opencode -s <id>` will\n4\tresume with the fabricated prior conversation visible to the model and rendered in the TUI.\n5\t\n6\tPinned environment:\n7\t- `opencode --version` → **1.18.5**. npm package: `opencode-ai`.\n8\t- Tested on macOS (darwin), zsh, default data dir `~/.local/share/opencode/`.\n9\t\n10\tEverything below was verified empirically against this exact build on 2026-08-02, by\n11\tfabricating payloads, importing them, listing/resuming the result, and rendering the resumed\n12\tsession in a real pty (`scripts/pty-run.exp`). Where a claim is *not* verified it says so.\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Storage: one SQLite DB, not per-session files\n17\t\n18\tUnlike Claude Code (`~/.claude/projects/<encoded-cwd>/<uuid>.jsonl`) and Codex\n19\t(`~/.codex/sessions/<date>/rollout-*.jsonl`), opencode keeps **all** sessions in one SQLite\n20\tdatabase: `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db`. Relevant tables: `session`, `session_message`,\n21\t`message`, `part`, `project`, `project_directory`.\n22\t\n23\tConsequence for fabrication: there is no \"write a new file into a directory the CLI scans\"\n24\tpath. The two options are writing SQLite directly (schema-coupled, racy against a running\n25\topencode) or going through opencode's own ingestion command. **turnbridge uses the latter.**\n26\t\n27\t## 2. Commands\n28\t\n29\t| Command | Behavior |\n30\t|---|---|\n31\t| `opencode -s <sessionID>` | Resume a session by id (TUI). Also `--session`. |\n32\t| `opencode -c` / `--continue` | Continue the *last* session. |\n33\t| `opencode export [sessionID]` | Dump a session as JSON to stdout. `--sanitize` redacts. |\n34\t| `opencode import <file>` | Load a session from a JSON file or share URL. |\n35\t| `opencode session list` | List sessions **for the current directory's project**. |\n36\t| `opencode session delete <sessionID>` | Delete a session. |\n37\t| `opencode debug scrap` | JSON list of all known projects (`id`, `worktree`, …). |\n38\t\n39\t`export` and `import` use the same envelope, so **a real exported session is the ground truth\n40\tfor what a fabricated one must look like**.\n41\t\n42\tVerified properties of `import`:\n43\t- On success it prints exactly `Imported session: <sessionID>` on stdout, exit 0.\n44\t- It honors `info.id` from the payload — the imported id is the one supplied, not a new one.\n45\t- It is **idempotent by id**: re-importing the same payload updates in place, no duplicate row.\n46\t- It is **not atomic**. A payload rejected part-way (see §5) leaves the messages accepted so far\n47\t  in the DB as a truncated session, visible in `session list`. Callers must clean up.\n48\t- Validation errors are reported as a path into the payload, e.g.\n49\t  `Error: Unexpected error / Missing key at [\"state\"][\"title\"]`, with no indication of which\n50\t  message or part was at fault.\n51\t\n52\tThere is **no argument-free \"open the session picker\" invocation** — `-s` always takes an id,\n53\t`-c` names a specific session, bare `opencode` starts a new one. This is why turnbridge installs\n54\tno opencode shim (see `src/shim.ts`).\n55\t\n56\t## 3. Envelope\n57\t\n58\t```jsonc\n59\t{\n60\t  \"info\": { /* session */ },\n61\t  \"messages\": [ { \"info\": { /* message */ }, \"parts\": [ /* parts */ ] } ]\n62\t}\n63\t```\n64\t\n65\t### 3.1 `info` (session)\n66\t\n67\tObserved on a real export:\n68\t\n69\t```jsonc\n70\t{\n71\t  \"id\": \"ses_042f83972ffeNdS75n197unLAD\",\n72\t  \"slug\": \"tidy-moon\",\n73\t  \"projectID\": \"5687eeea165450405ca0226fde0ab28e91701579\",\n74\t  \"directory\": \"/Users/me/repos/turnbridge\",\n75\t  \"path\": \"\",\n76\t  \"title\": \"Turnbridge opencode support check\",\n77\t  \"agent\": \"build\",\n78\t  \"model\": { \"id\": \"deepseek-v4-flash\", \"providerID\": \"ds4\" },\n79\t  \"version\": \"1.18.5\",\n80\t  \"summary\": { \"additions\": 0, \"deletions\": 0, \"files\": 0 },\n81\t  \"cost\": 0,\n82\t  \"tokens\": { \"input\": 0, \"output\": 0, \"reasoning\": 0, \"cache\": { \"read\": 0, \"write\": 0 } },\n83\t  \"time\": { \"created\": 1785582831245, \"updated\": 1785594863222 }\n84\t}\n85\t```\n86\t\n87\t- `id` — arbitrary string, not a UUID. Native ids are `ses_<base62>`; turnbridge writes\n88\t  `ses_tb_<uuid>` so bridged sessions are identifiable on sight. Accepted without complaint.\n89\t- **`projectID` is load-bearing and is honored verbatim.** It scopes which directory lists the\n90\t  session. Ids are opaque hashes stored in the `project` table — *not* a plain sha1 of the\n91\t  worktree path (checked: sha1 of the path, path+newline, and lowercased path all mismatch), so\n92\t  they must be **looked up**, via `opencode debug scrap`, matching `worktree` against the launch\n93\t  cwd. `\"global\"` is a real fallback project (worktree `/`); a session filed there is listed\n94\t  from **every** directory, which is why turnbridge only uses it when the cwd maps to no\n95\t  registered project.\n96\t- `title` — what `session list` and the TUI picker display. Fabricated sessions title themselves\n97\t  by the first human message, not by the import notice (same reasoning as the other targets).\n98\t- `time.updated` is the picker's sort key; turnbridge stamps it `now` so a freshly bridged\n99\t  session sorts to the top even though its *messages* are backdated (§4).\n100\t- `permission` was written by an earlier draft of the adapter and is **not** present on real\n101\t  exports; it imports fine but is not part of the format.\n102\t\n103\t### 3.2 `info` (message)\n104\t\n105\tUser:\n106\t```jsonc\n107\t{ \"role\": \"user\", \"time\": { \"created\": 1785582831260 }, \"agent\": \"build\",\n108\t  \"model\": { \"providerID\": \"ds4\", \"modelID\": \"deepseek-v4-flash\" },\n109\t  \"summary\": { \"diffs\": [] }, \"id\": \"msg_…\", \"sessionID\": \"ses_…\" }\n110\t```\n111\t\n112\tAssistant:\n113\t```jsonc\n114\t{ \"role\": \"assistant\", \"parentID\": \"msg_…\", \"mode\": \"build\", \"agent\": \"build\",\n115\t  \"path\": { \"cwd\": \"…\", \"root\": \"…\" }, \"cost\": 0,\n116\t  \"tokens\": { \"total\": 7918, \"input\": 7601, \"output\": 317, \"reasoning\": 0,\n117\t              \"cache\": { \"write\": 0, \"read\": 0 } },\n118\t  \"modelID\": \"deepseek-v4-flash\", \"providerID\": \"ds4\",\n119\t  \"time\": { \"created\": …, \"completed\": … }, \"finish\": \"tool-calls\",\n120\t  \"id\": \"msg_…\", \"sessionID\": \"ses_…\" }\n121\t```\n122\t\n123\t**`parentID` semantics** (measured on a real 98-message export): user messages carry **no**\n124\t`parentID`; each assistant message points at **the user message that prompted it**, so several\n125\tconsecutive assistant messages share one parent. It is a prompt→replies tree, not a linked\n126\tlist. Chaining every message to its predecessor (an earlier draft) parents user messages to\n127\tassistant messages, which no native session does.\n128\t\n129\t## 4. Message ordering is by `time.created`, not payload order\n130\t\n131\tThe rendered order comes from the timestamps, not the array. Practical consequence, observed:\n132\tan import notice stamped at fabrication time is *newer* than every backdated history message, so\n133\tit renders **last**, and a trailing user message with no assistant reply renders with a\n134\t**`QUEUED`** badge — i.e. it reads as a prompt about to be sent rather than a preamble.\n135\t\n136\tThis is the opposite of the Claude Code target, where the notice is deliberately stamped `now`\n137\tbecause that format renders in file order. turnbridge therefore backdates the opencode notice to\n138\t`earliest_event_ms - 1`. Verified: notice renders first, no `QUEUED` badge.\n139\t\n140\t## 5. Parts\n141\t\n142\tObserved types: `text`, `reasoning`, `tool`, `step-start`, `step-finish`, `patch`.\n143\t\n144\tEvery part carries `id` (`prt_…`), `sessionID`, `messageID`.\n145\t\n146\t- **`text`** — `{ type, text, id, sessionID, messageID }`. Minimal and always accepted.\n147\t- **`reasoning`** — as `text` plus `time: { start, end }`. Renders as a collapsed\n148\t  `+ Thought: <duration>` line.\n149\t- **`step-start`** — real assistant messages begin with one (carrying a `snapshot` git sha).\n150\t  **Not required**: fabricated assistant messages with no `step-start` render their text\n151\t  normally. turnbridge omits it rather than inventing a snapshot id.\n152\t- **`tool`** — `{ type: \"tool\", tool: <name>, callID, state, … }`. **`state` requires all six of\n153\t  `status`, `input`, `output`, `title`, `metadata`, `time`.** Omitting any one fails the entire\n\n... [93 lines truncated] ...","type":"edited_text_file"},"injection_type":"attachment/edited_text_file"},"context":{"branch":"fix/opencode-continuation","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"77375496d5ae42be257ec16d189b3204a09dd681","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"id":"ev2-12107a96ee268380b5f6dce4216e9af9c022ce796804142858788427f8a8b279","kind":"context_injection","meta":{"actor":{"type":"system"},"session_id":"4fc5e727-a1d5-4399-a448-9d24a0ce9c15","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.227"},"occurred_at":"2026-08-11T11:00:11.942Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"attachment":{"filename":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md","snippet":"1\t# opencode native session / import format — reverse-engineering spec\n2\t\n3\tPurpose: enable `turnbridge` to FABRICATE an opencode session that `opencode -s <id>` will\n4\tresume with the fabricated prior conversation visible to the model and rendered in the TUI.\n5\t\n6\tPinned environment:\n7\t- `opencode --version` → **1.18.5**. npm package: `opencode-ai`.\n8\t- Tested on macOS (darwin), zsh, default data d