{"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01LYgkL6KxGEJLgQbaojf2ao","input":{"command":"git push -q -u origin fix/opencode-continuation 2>&1 | tail -2; gh pr create --base docs/fabrication-tiers-and-opencode-provider-bug --title \"opencode: make bridged sessions continuable\" --body \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nStacked on #7 (the contract change this implements). Merge #7 first and this retargets to `main`.\n\n## The bug\n\nA fabricated opencode session rendered perfectly and could not be talked to. Three defects, each invisible on screen, found by asking a resumed session to recall a marker planted in its bridged history.\n\n### 1. `providerID` was a provenance value in a dispatch field\n\nThe adapter wrote `providerID: \"turnbridge\"`, per the old contract rule to never substitute a recognized-but-false model id. opencode resolves its agent loop's model from **what the session stores** — not from `-m`, not from the TUI model picker — so it threw `ProviderModelNotFoundError` and exited at step 0 without issuing a request.\n\nSymptoms varied by scoping, which is part of why it hid: under a registered project `opencode run -s` exited **0 with empty output** and the TUI silently swallowed the prompt; under `global` it surfaced as `UnknownError`.\n\nNow the source model is resolved against `opencode models`: kept verbatim when opencode can run it (opencode→opencode keeps its own model), otherwise substituted and disclosed both in the import notice and in a launch note. With nothing listed, fabrication refuses and falls back to bootstrap rather than writing a session that cannot dispatch.\n\n### 2. Ids are an ordering key\n\n- **Parts render in `id` order.** `prt_tb_<uuid>` scrambled them the moment assistant messages gained step parts — exports showed `step-finish` ahead of `step-start`. Invisible before, because messages had exactly one part.\n- **Message ids must sort ahead of what opencode mints next.** Native ids are time-ascending base62, presently in the `f` range; `msg_tb_<uuid>` sorts after them, so every fabricated message ranked *after* the reply opencode was about to write. A resumed session then answered and **kept generating** — 10–13 assistant turns for one prompt — where a native session in the same directory, project and model answered once in 12.6s.\n\nIds are now minted in emission order, message ids below anything opencode will mint.\n\n### 3. Native part shape\n\nAssistant messages carried a bare `text` part; native ones are wrapped in `step-start … step-finish`. The spec called `step-start` \"not required\" on the strength of *rendering*, which was the wrong test — the TUI and the agent loop are built around those parts. Emitted now, with the launch repo's `HEAD` as `snapshot`.\n\n## Verification\n\nEnd to end against opencode 1.18.5, with a real provider: a 4-turn Claude Code conversation bridged into opencode answers a question about its own history — **exit 0, 25s, one assistant turn**, correct marker. Controls run throughout: a native session with the same model in the same directory answered once in 12.6s both before and after.\n\n`-m` also overrides the stored model again (session stored `big-pickle`, ran on `deepseek-v4-flash-free`), which it could not do while the provider was unresolvable — so turnbridge's model pick is a starting default the user can change.\n\n91 tests pass (was 88; +3 covering provider resolution, notice disclosure, and step wrapping, and one rewritten that had asserted the broken behavior).\n\n## Not in this PR\n\nThe two probes (headless model-recall, 300-turn history) — the gaps they close are real and still open. Codex 0.147.0 drift is also untouched.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01XvcBwjacvqTAFGdaMhEcei\nEOF\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -3","description":"Push and open stacked PR","timeout":180000},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"fix/opencode-continuation","cwd":"/Use