{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"You're right, and here's exactly what happened.\n\n**#7** merged to main correctly, so the docs are there. **#8** merged into `docs/fabrication-tiers-and-opencode-provider-bug` — its base branch — not into main. GitHub only auto-retargets a stacked PR when the base branch is *deleted* at merge time; #7's merge kept the branch alive, so #8 had a live base to merge into and did. That was my call to stack it, so the extra step is on me.\n\nNet effect: main has the contract docs but **not the fix**. Commit `7737549` currently lives only on the two feature branches.\n\n**PR #9** is open — `fix/opencode-continuation` → `main`. Since #7's docs commit is already on main, the delta is exactly the one fix commit:\n\n```\ndocs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md            8 +-\ndocs/specs/opencode-session-format.md  45 ++-\nsrc/targets/opencode.ts               200 +++++++++--\nsrc/test/opencode-fabricate.test.ts    86 ++++--\n```\n\nMerge that and main is complete. I'd let it delete the branch this time.\n\nOne consequence worth knowing while #9 sits unmerged: your global `turnbridge`/`tb` is npm-linked to the repo working tree, not to main — so whichever branch is checked out is what runs. I'm currently on `fix/opencode-continuation` with the fix built into `dist/`, so bridging into opencode works right now. But if you check out main before merging #9, you'd be back on the broken adapter. Merging #9 makes that moot.\n\nAfter merge, `git checkout main && git pull && npm run build` puts you on the merged state cleanly.\n\nresult: Diagnosed why #8 didn't retarget — it merged into its still-existing base branch, leaving the fix commit off main — and opened PR #9 (`fix/opencode-continuation` → `main`) containing exactly that one commit; merging it completes main.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"fix/opencode-continuation","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"77375496d5ae42be257ec16d189b3204a09dd681","repo":"g